// HTML, CSS and JavaScript course final MCQ (web-client-mcq), graded, 30 questions, 30 minutes. Published in this repository under the captain's public-answer consent (Firstmate, 2026-11-19).

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/assessments/web-client-mcq

::html-css-js-final-mcq-01::[html]<p>A loop sums prices. Execution is paused on the loop body with Scope showing <code>i\: 3</code>, <code>total\: 27</code> and items holding 3 entries. You press Step over.</p><pre><code>for (let i \= 0; i &lt;\= items.length; i++) \{
&nbsp; total +\= items[i].price;
\}</code></pre><p>What happens, and what does it tell you?</p> {
=A TypeError\: items[3] is undefined, so the condition should use &lt; not &lt;\=#Correct. Indexes run from 0 to length - 1. With &lt;\= the loop makes one extra pass, and reading .price on undefined throws.
~total becomes NaN and the loop ends normally, so the prices hold a text value#Not quite. Reading a property of undefined throws a TypeError; it does not produce NaN.
~Step over skips the line because i is past the end, so the loop is fine#Not quite. Step over runs the paused line; nothing skips it, and the extra pass is the bug.
~items[3] wraps round to items[0], so the first price is counted twice#Not quite. Arrays do not wrap; an index past the end gives undefined.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-02::[html]<p>A blog post is wrapped in an article. Its last lines read "Written by Ann Lee, 12 May 2026. Contact\: ann@example.com" and close the post.</p><p>Which element should wrap those last lines?</p> {
~aside#Not quite. aside is for content only loosely related to what is around it. These lines close the post itself.
=footer#Correct. footer holds closing information for the page or its section, such as authorship and contact details, and it can close an article.
~section#Not quite. section is a thematic group of content, usually with its own heading. Closing details for the post belong in its footer.
~header#Not quite. header introduces a page or section; these lines close the post rather than open it.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-03::[html]<p>A gallery container has <code>display\: grid</code>, <code>gap\: 16px</code> and a 900px-wide content box. Each cell holds one image with <code>width\: 100%</code>.</p><p>Which <code>grid-template-columns</code> value gives exactly four columns with no sideways scrolling?</p> {
~<code>repeat(4, 225px)</code>#Not quite. Four 225px columns already take 900px, and the three 16px gaps come on top, so the grid is 948px wide and overflows.
~<code>repeat(auto-fill, 225px)</code>#Not quite. auto-fill makes as many 225px columns as fit. With the gaps, a fourth needs 948px, so only three fit.
=<code>repeat(4, 1fr)</code>#Correct. fr shares the space left after the three 16px gaps, so four columns of 213px each fit inside 900px.
~<code>4fr</code>#Not quite. This defines one track that takes all the free space, so the images stack in a single column.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-04::[html]<p>Three blocks sit in normal flow with the default <code>box-sizing\: content-box</code> and no borders. A has <code>margin-bottom\: 20px</code>. B has <code>margin-top\: 10px</code>, <code>padding\: 8px</code>, a content height of 50px and <code>margin-bottom\: 40px</code>. C has <code>margin-top\: 25px</code>.</p><p>How far is it from A's bottom border edge to C's top border edge?</p> {
~161px#Not quite. That adds every margin. Adjoining vertical margins between siblings collapse to the larger one.
=126px#Correct. 20px and 10px collapse to 20px, B's border box is 50 + 8 + 8 \= 66px, and 40px and 25px collapse to 40px.
~110px#Not quite. The margins are right, but B's padding is part of its box\: 50px of content plus 8px top and bottom is 66px.
~101px#Not quite. Collapsed margins take the larger value, not the smaller\: 20px and 40px, not 10px and 25px.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-05::[html]<p>This script runs in Node.js.</p><pre><code>let step \= 1;
for (let i \= 1; i &lt;\= 3; i++) setTimeout(() \=&gt; console.log(i * step), 0);
step \= 10;</code></pre><p>What is logged, one value per line?</p> {
~1, 2, 3#Not quite. A closure keeps the variable, not a snapshot of its value. step is already 10 when the timers fire.
=10, 20, 30#Correct. Each callback keeps its own i through let, and it reads step when it runs, after the loop and step \= 10 have finished.
~40, 40, 40#Not quite. let gives every iteration its own i, so the callbacks see 1, 2 and 3, not the final 4.
~4, 4, 4#Not quite. Each iteration has its own i, and step is 10 by the time any callback runs.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-06::[html]<p>This runs in Node.js.</p><pre><code>const cart \= \{ items\: [] \};
function add(c, item) \{ c.items.push(item); c \= \{ items\: [] \}; \}
add(cart, "pen");
console.log(cart.items.length);</code></pre><p>What happens?</p> {
~It prints 0#Not quite. Assigning a new object to c changes only the parameter, not the cart binding outside, so cart still holds the pushed item.
~A TypeError, because add reassigns the const cart#Not quite. c is the function's own parameter, not the const cart; reassigning it is allowed.
~A TypeError at push, because a const object cannot be changed#Not quite. const stops reassigning the name, not changing the object; push works.
=It prints 1#Correct. c starts as a second name for the same object, so push changes cart. Reassigning the parameter c then only rebinds c; cart and its const binding are untouched.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-07::[html]<p>This runs in Node.js.</p><pre><code>const rows \= [\{ price\: 5 \}, \{ qty\: 0, price\: 9 \}, \{ qty\: 2, price\: 4 \}];
const total \= rows
&nbsp; .map((\{ qty \= 1, price \}) \=&gt; qty * price)
&nbsp; .reduce((sum, n) \=&gt; sum + n, 0);</code></pre><p>What is total?</p> {
~22#Not quite. A default value replaces only undefined, not 0, so the second row contributes 0, not 9.
~NaN#Not quite. The default value fills the missing qty with 1, so the first row is 5, not undefined x 5.
=13#Correct. The default applies only when qty is missing\: 1 x 5, then 0 x 9 \= 0, then 2 x 4 \= 8, and reduce adds them to 13.
~[5, 0, 8]#Not quite. That is the array map returns. reduce then adds its values to a single number.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-08::[html]<p>The <code>.nav</code> row has no gap, padding or borders. Its three links hold short text, so no minimum size stops them shrinking. The middle link has the class <code>mid</code>.</p><pre><code>.nav \{ display\: flex; flex-direction\: row; width\: 600px; \}
.nav a \{ flex-basis\: 300px; flex-shrink\: 1; \}
.nav .mid \{ flex-shrink\: 2; \}</code></pre><p>How wide is the middle link?</p> {
~200px#Not quite. That splits the 600px evenly and ignores flex-shrink, which decides how the 300px overflow is shared.
~225px#Not quite. That is an outer link's width. The middle link has the larger flex-shrink, so it shrinks most\: 150px.
=150px#Correct. The bases total 900px, 300px too many. With equal bases, the overflow is shared 1 \: 2 \: 1, so the middle link loses 150px.
~300px#Not quite. Flex items shrink when their bases overflow the container, because flex-shrink is not 0.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-09::[html]<p>Client code builds request URLs from <code>import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL</code>, which is defined only in <code>.env.development</code>. After <code>npm run build</code> and a deploy, every request goes to a path that starts with <code>undefined</code>.</p><p>What is the cause and fix?</p> {
~Vite exposes env values only while the dev server runs; read process.env.VITE_API_URL in client code instead#Not quite. Client code reads import.meta.env in every mode. The build replaces it with the value from the files that mode loads.
~Production builds drop the VITE_ prefix; rename the variable to API_URL in .env.development#Not quite. The VITE_ prefix is what exposes a variable to client code in every mode. Without it the value is never exposed.
~Define VITE_API_URL on the web server that serves the files, and the deployed bundle reads it when it loads#Not quite. Vite inlines the value into the bundle as text at build time. The browser never reads the server's environment.
=The build uses production mode, which skips .env.development; set it in .env.production and rebuild#Correct. Mode files apply only in their mode, and Vite inlines the value at build time. A production build needs the variable in a file that mode reads.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-10::[html]<p>A teammate changes a dependency range in package.json and runs <code>npm install</code>, which updates package-lock.json. They commit only package.json. On the build machine, <code>npm ci</code> now stops with an error that the lockfile does not match package.json.</p><p>What is the cause and fix?</p> {
=The committed lockfile is stale; commit the package-lock.json that npm install updated#Correct. npm ci installs exactly what the lockfile records and refuses to run when it disagrees with package.json. The updated lockfile fixes it.
~The new range is invalid for npm ci; pin an exact version in package.json instead#Not quite. A range is fine for npm ci. The problem is that the committed lockfile was written for the old package.json.
~The build machine needs the packages themselves; commit the node_modules folder as well#Not quite. node_modules is rebuilt from the lockfile and is not committed. The stale lockfile is what npm ci rejects.
~npm ci cannot work with a lockfile; delete package-lock.json so the build installs fresh#Not quite. npm ci requires a lockfile and installs exactly from it. Deleting it makes npm ci fail outright.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-11::[html]<p>A booking form uses <code>required</code>, <code>type\="date"</code> and <code>min</code>, and all browser checks pass. The Spring Boot API still answers 400 with problem details\: the night is in the venue's blackout dates, a rule only the server holds, and the API is specified to answer 400 for it.</p><p>What should a reviewer ask for?</p> {
~Add a pattern attribute that lists the blackout dates so the browser blocks them#Not quite. The blackout list lives on the server and can change. Copying a server rule into markup does not keep the server's answer from mattering.
=Keep the HTML checks and show the API's problem detail by the date field on a 400#Correct. Browser checks are a convenience; the server stays the authority. The client should show the server's reason so the user can pick another night.
~Remove the HTML checks, because the server validates everything anyway#Not quite. The browser checks give fast feedback on simple errors. Server validation is needed in addition, not instead.
~Treat the 400 like a network failure and show a generic message asking the user to retry#Not quite. Retrying the same date fails the same way. A 400 with problem details says what to fix.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-12::[html]<p>Staging serves the page and the API together at http\://staging.example.com, plain HTTP. Login answers 200 with a session cookie marked <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code> and <code>SameSite\=Strict</code>, yet every later request from the staging page arrives at the API without a session.</p><p>What is the cause?</p> {
~HttpOnly hides the cookie from the server as well as from page script#Not quite. HttpOnly hides the cookie from page script only; the browser still sends it to the server.
~SameSite\=Strict blocks it, because the staging page and its API are treated as other sites#Not quite. Requests the staging page starts to its own site are same-site; SameSite limits requests other sites start.
~The page must copy the cookie into localStorage and send it in a header#Not quite. Page script cannot read an HttpOnly cookie, and copying a token into localStorage is the risk the cookie avoids.
=Secure keeps the cookie off plain HTTP, so staging over http never gets it sent#Correct. A Secure cookie travels only over HTTPS; browsers do not even keep one that arrives over plain HTTP. Serve staging over HTTPS, as production is, instead of dropping the attribute.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-13::[html]<p>A Vitest test fails with Expected 0.3 and Received 0.30000000000000004.</p><pre><code>const add \= (a, b) \=&gt; a + b;
expect(add(0.1, 0.2)).toBe(0.3);</code></pre><p>Which statement is right?</p> {
~toBe compares identity, so switching the assertion to toEqual makes the test pass#Not quite. For numbers, toBe and toEqual both compare the value, and the values differ.
~Vitest printed the values the wrong way round; 0.3 is what add returned#Not quite. Received is always the value your code produced; Expected is the value in toBe.
~add joined the numbers as text, so the test should compare with a string#Not quite. + on two numbers adds them; the long decimal is a float result, not text.
=The value is real\: 0.1 + 0.2 is a 64-bit float sum, and toEqual would fail too#Correct. Received is what the code produced. 0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3, and toEqual compares the same two numbers.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-14::[html]<p>One click listener on a <code>ul</code> handles the Details links inside its list items\: it calls <code>preventDefault()</code> and opens a panel. A teammate then adds a click listener to each <code>li</code> that highlights the row and calls <code>stopPropagation()</code>. Now clicking Details follows the link instead of opening the panel.</p><p>What is the cause?</p> {
~Clicks travel from the ul down to the link, so the ul listener runs after the link was already followed#Not quite. Listeners added with addEventListener run in the bubbling phase, from the target up. The ul would run after the li, if the event reached it.
~Adding an li listener makes the li the event target, so the ul listener no longer matches the link#Not quite. event.target stays the element where the click started, the link. The ul listener simply never receives the event.
=The li listener stops the click rising, so the ul listener never runs and nothing calls preventDefault#Correct. Delegation relies on the click bubbling from the link up to the ul. stopPropagation on the li ends the rise there.
~An element can hold only one click listener, so the new li listeners replace the ul's listener#Not quite. addEventListener adds listeners; it never replaces another element's listener. The ul listener is still there.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-15::[html]<p>At 375px the page scrolls sideways. The base styles below make a row of three cards that cannot shrink. The cards should stack on phones and sit in a row from 960px.</p><pre><code>.cards \{ display\: flex; \}
.card \{ flex-basis\: 300px; flex-shrink\: 0; \}</code></pre><p>Which change fixes it mobile-first?</p> {
~Add <code>@media (min-width\: 960px) \{ .cards \{ flex-direction\: column; \} \}</code> and keep the base styles unchanged#Not quite. A min-width query applies at 960px and wider, so this stacks the wide layout and leaves the phone row overflowing.
~Add the viewport meta element with <code>width\=device-width</code> in the head and keep all of the CSS as it is#Not quite. The viewport meta makes the phone lay the page out at its real width; it does not change the 900px row, which still overflows.
=Base <code>flex-direction\: column</code> on .cards, plus <code>@media (min-width\: 960px) \{ .cards \{ flex-direction\: row; \} \}</code>#Correct. Mobile-first puts the phone layout in the base styles and adds the wide layout with a min-width query, so phones stack and wide screens get the row.
~Add <code>width\: 100%</code> to .cards so that the row can never grow wider than the phone's screen#Not quite. The container is already full width; the cards inside still need 900px and cannot shrink, so they overflow it.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-16::[html]<p>A profile card builds a link from a numeric user id sent by the server and a display name the visitor typed.</p><pre><code>card.innerHTML \= `&lt;a href\="/users/$\{user.id\}"&gt;$\{nameInput.value\}&lt;/a&gt;`;</code></pre><p>Which rewrite keeps the link and removes the risk?</p> {
~Keep innerHTML but wrap the name in a span element inside the link#Not quite. Markup around the name does not stop markup inside it. Any tag the visitor types is still parsed as HTML.
=Build the a with createElement, set href from user.id and textContent to the name, and append it#Correct. Building the element and setting textContent means the name is only ever treated as text, while the link still works.
~Assign the same template to card.textContent instead of card.innerHTML#Not quite. That is safe but shows the whole template as plain text, so the card no longer has a working link.
~Create the a element and set its href from user.id as before, but put the name in with innerHTML#Not quite. Assigning the visitor's text to innerHTML on the new element still parses it as HTML.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-17::[html]<p>The page has <code>\#status</code> and a list <code>list</code>, but no element with id count. <code>item</code> is a new li element. <code>user.displayName</code> contains HTML tags.</p><pre><code>const status \= document.querySelector("\#status");
status.classList.add("ready"); status.textContent \= user.displayName;
document.querySelector("\#count").textContent \= "3";
list.append(item);</code></pre><p>What does the page show after the script runs?</p> {
~\#status shows the tags as text and the item is added; line 3 quietly does nothing#Not quite. querySelector returns null when nothing matches, and setting textContent on null throws, so line 4 never runs.
~\#status renders the tags as HTML elements; line 3 throws, so the list item is never added#Not quite. textContent inserts text only; the tags appear literally and are never parsed.
=\#status gets the class and shows the tags as text; line 3 throws, so the item is not added#Correct. textContent never parses markup. querySelector returns null for a missing id, and setting a property on null throws a TypeError that stops the script.
~\#status shows the tags as text but loses the ready class, and the list item is added#Not quite. Setting textContent changes the children, not the class list, and line 3 still throws first.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-18::[html]<p>main.js and prices.js work under the Vite dev server. Copied unchanged to a plain local static server, where index.html already loads main.js with <code>type\="module"</code>, the page cannot load prices.js. main.js begins like this.</p><pre><code>import \{ formatPrice \} from "./prices";</code></pre><p>What is the cause and fix?</p> {
=The browser does not add a missing .js extension as Vite does; import from "./prices.js"#Correct. Vite resolves extension-less paths for you. The browser requests the path exactly as written, and /prices does not exist on the static server.
~Module scripts break on a server; open index.html straight from the file system instead#Not quite. It is the other way round\: module scripts follow CORS rules, so file\:// usually fails and a server is required.
~Browsers load only default exports; change prices.js to export formatPrice as default#Not quite. Browsers support named imports and exports. The import cannot find the file, not the name.
~Drop the leading ./ so the browser looks up prices from the site root#Not quite. Without ./ the browser treats prices as a bare module name and refuses it. The path needs ./ and the .js extension.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-19::[html]<p>A Java method <code>int pages \= items / perPage;</code> is ported to a Node.js file like this.</p><pre><code>const items \= 7, perPage \= 2;
const pages \= items / perPage;
console.log(pages);
for (let p \= 1; p &lt;\= pages; p++) console.log(p);</code></pre><p>What does the file print?</p> {
~3, then 1, 2 and 3#Not quite. There is no int type to truncate the result; Number division keeps the fraction.
~4, then 1, 2, 3 and 4#Not quite. Division does not round up; 7 / 2 is exactly 3.5.
~3.5, then 1, 2, 3 and 4#Not quite. 4 &lt;\= 3.5 is false, so the loop stops after 3.
=3.5, then 1, 2 and 3#Correct. JavaScript has one Number type, so 7 / 2 is 3.5, not integer division. The loop stops when p reaches 4, which is above 3.5.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-20::[html]<p>A page shell must be one column on phones. From 900px wide, the header must run across the top with the sidebar beside the main area. These base rules are shared.</p><pre><code>.shell \{ display\: grid; grid-template-areas\: "head" "side" "main"; \}
header \{ grid-area\: head; \} aside \{ grid-area\: side; \} main \{ grid-area\: main; \}</code></pre><p>Which rule set produces that wide layout and keeps phones at one column?</p> {
~<code>@media (max-width\: 900px) \{ .shell \{ grid-template-columns\: 240px 1fr; grid-template-areas\: "head head" "side main"; \} \}</code>#Not quite. max-width applies the two-column layout to narrow screens, so phones get the sidebar layout and wide screens keep one column.
~<code>@media (min-width\: 900px) \{ .shell \{ grid-template-columns\: 240px 1fr; grid-template-areas\: "head side" "main main"; \} \}</code>#Not quite. These areas put the sidebar in the top row beside the header and stretch main across the bottom.
=<code>@media (min-width\: 900px) \{ .shell \{ grid-template-columns\: 240px 1fr; grid-template-areas\: "head head" "side main"; \} \}</code>#Correct. Mobile-first\: the one-column base applies everywhere, and the min-width query adds two columns with the header spanning both from 900px.
~<code>@media (min-width\: 900px) \{ .shell \{ grid-template-columns\: 240px 1fr; \} \}</code>#Not quite. The areas still name one cell per row, so header, sidebar and main stay stacked in the first column and the second column is empty.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-21::[html]<p>This runs in Node.js.</p><pre><code>async function load(id) \{ if (!id) throw new Error("missing id"); \}
const p \= load();
console.log("after call");
p.catch((err) \=&gt; console.log("caught", err.message));</code></pre><p>What is logged?</p> {
=after call, then caught missing id#Correct. A throw inside an async function rejects its promise instead of stopping the caller, so the log runs first and the catch handler runs later.
~caught missing id, then after call#Not quite. Handlers run only after the current code finishes, so the synchronous log comes first.
~An uncaught Error at load(), and nothing is logged#Not quite. An async function never throws to its caller; the error becomes a rejected promise.
~after call, then an unhandled rejection, because catch was attached after the throw#Not quite. The handler is attached in the same run of code, before the rejection is reported, so it catches it.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-22::[html]<p>An async click handler awaits a stubbed request, then loops over a large array for several seconds. During that loop other buttons and a text field do not respond. A teammate says the await should already keep the page responsive.</p><p>Which statement is right?</p> {
~Adding one more await just before the loop would let queued clicks run between its iterations#Not quite. One await pauses once, before the loop. The loop itself still runs to the end without giving the thread back.
~After an await the rest of the handler runs on a background thread, so the freeze comes from somewhere else#Not quite. JavaScript on the page runs on one main thread. Resumed async code shares it with event handling and rendering.
~The browser runs queued clicks between loop iterations, so the stubbed request must cause the freeze#Not quite. The event loop starts the next task only when the current one finishes. A loop iteration is not a task boundary.
=The await frees the thread only while the request is pending; the loop then holds it until it ends#Correct. Code after an await resumes as a microtask on the single main thread, and nothing interrupts running code. A long synchronous loop holds that thread, so queued clicks and typing wait until it ends.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-23::[html]<p>A keyboard walk-through of a settings panel logs this. Theme dialog\: Tab cycles inside it; Escape closes it and returns focus to the button that opened it. Brightness\: a div with tabindex\="0" that Tab focuses; pressing arrow keys or Space leaves its value unchanged. Help\: an a element with an href, holding only an icon, with aria-label\="Help"; Tab reaches it.</p><p>Which note records a real barrier?</p> {
=Brightness\: keyboard users cannot change it, so use a native element or add key handling#Correct. Every control must be reachable and operable with the keyboard alone. Being focusable is not enough when no key changes the value.
~The dialog\: focus cycling inside it is a keyboard trap, even though Escape closes it#Not quite. A keyboard trap is focus that Tab, Shift+Tab or Escape cannot move out. Escape closes this dialog and returns focus, so it is not a trap.
~Help\: an aria-label cannot name a link, so the icon link needs visible text to have a name#Not quite. aria-label gives an icon-only control its accessible name, so the link is named.
~Help\: like a form field, the link needs a label element whose for attribute points at it#Not quite. label elements name form fields. A link gets its name from its text or an aria-label.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-24::[html]<p>This code logs undefined once per book and throws no error.</p><pre><code>const books \= [\{ title\: "Dune" \}, \{ title\: "Emma" \}];
const toItem \= (book) \=&gt; (\{ label\: book.title \});
books.map(toItem).forEach((\{ title \}) \=&gt; console.log(title));</code></pre><p>Which version of the last line logs each title?</p> {
~<code>books.map(toItem()).forEach((\{ title \}) \=&gt; console.log(title));</code>#Not quite. toItem() calls the function with no book and fails; map needs the function itself.
=<code>books.map(toItem).forEach((\{ label \}) \=&gt; console.log(label));</code>#Correct. toItem builds objects with a label property, so the pattern must name label to pull the title out.
~<code>books.forEach(toItem).forEach((\{ title \}) \=&gt; console.log(title));</code>#Not quite. forEach returns undefined, so the second forEach throws a TypeError.
~<code>books.map((book) \=&gt; \{ label\: book.title \}).forEach((\{ title \}) \=&gt; console.log(title));</code>#Not quite. Braces after the arrow start a block, so the callback returns undefined and destructuring it throws.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-25::[html]<p>On a 400 response the page still shows Saved.</p><pre><code>async function save(draft) \{
&nbsp; const res \= await fetch("/api/drafts", \{ method\: "POST",
&nbsp;   headers\: \{ "Content-Type"\: "application/json" \}, body\: JSON.stringify(draft) \});
&nbsp; if (res.ok) return \{ ok\: true \};
&nbsp; return \{ ok\: false, status\: res.status \}; \}
try \{ await save(draft); showSaved(); \} catch (err) \{ showError(); \}</code></pre><p>What is the cause?</p> {
~fetch rejects on a 400, but the try block ends before the rejection arrives#Not quite. fetch rejects only when no response arrives; a 400 is a response.
=save turns a 400 into a result object, and the caller never checks it#Correct. fetch fulfils on a 400, and save turns it into \{ ok\: false \} rather than a rejection. The caller has to read result.ok to choose the message.
~save is missing await on the return line, so the result is still a promise#Not quite. save is async and its return value is a plain object; await in the caller already unwraps it.
~The 400 body is problem details JSON, which makes showSaved run first#Not quite. The body is never read here; the caller simply ignores what save returned.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-26::[html]<p>This runs in Node.js.</p><pre><code>Promise.resolve(2)
&nbsp; .then((n) \=&gt; n * 3)
&nbsp; .then((n) \=&gt; \{ if (n &gt; 5) throw new Error("too big " + n); return n; \})
&nbsp; .then((n) \=&gt; console.log("A", n))
&nbsp; .catch((err) \=&gt; console.log("B", err.message));</code></pre><p>What is logged?</p> {
=B too big 6#Correct. Each then receives what the previous callback returned, so n is 6 in the check. The throw rejects the chain, skips the A step and runs catch.
~B too big 2#Not quite. A then callback receives the value the previous callback returned, not the original 2, so the check sees 6.
~A 6#Not quite. 6 is greater than 5, so the callback throws; a throw becomes a rejection, which skips the next then.
~A 6, then B too big 6#Not quite. A rejection skips every then until the next catch, so A never runs.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-27::[html]<p><code>wait(ms)</code> returns a promise that fulfills after ms milliseconds, and <code>fail(ms)</code> returns one that rejects after ms milliseconds.</p><pre><code>async function load() \{
&nbsp; try \{ await wait(100); await fail(200); await wait(300); \}
&nbsp; catch (err) \{ console.log("failed\:", err.message); \}
\}</code></pre><p>About how long after load() is called does the catch block run, and does the third call start?</p> {
=After about 300 ms; the third call never starts#Correct. Each await finishes before the next call begins\: 100 ms, then a rejection 200 ms later. The throw jumps to catch, so wait(300) is never called.
~After about 200 ms; the third call never starts#Not quite. The calls run one after another, not at the same time, so the rejection comes 100 + 200 ms after start.
~After about 200 ms; the third call has already started#Not quite. Sequential awaits do not start later calls early. Only starting them together, as with Promise.all, would.
~After about 600 ms, once all three calls have settled#Not quite. A rejected await throws at once, skipping the rest of the try block.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-28::[html]<p>A helper builds fetch options as <code>\{ ...defaults, ...options \}</code>, where <code>defaults.headers</code> sets Content-Type to application/json. A caller passes <code>\{ method\: "POST", body\: JSON.stringify(order), headers\: \{ Authorization\: token \} \}</code>. The Network panel shows the request sent as text/plain;charset\=UTF-8, and the API answers 415 with problem details.</p><p>What is the cause?</p> {
~Spread merges the nested headers, so the token is fine; the server is rejecting the Authorization value#Not quite. Spread does not merge nested objects. A rejected token would bring 401, not 415 Unsupported Media Type.
=Spread is shallow, so the caller's headers object replaces the default one and Content-Type is lost#Correct. Only top-level keys are merged. Without a Content-Type, fetch labels a string body text/plain, which the JSON endpoint rejects with 415.
~fetch always sends a string body as text/plain; pass the order object itself as the body#Not quite. fetch uses a Content-Type you set. Passing the object sends the text [object Object], which is worse.
~415 means the session expired; refresh the token and retry the same request#Not quite. 415 is Unsupported Media Type\: the server cannot read the body's declared type. An expired session is 401.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-29::[html]<p>In development the client calls relative /api paths through Vite's server.proxy, and everything works. The built files are then served by a plain static server on port 4000 with nothing else configured. Every /api request now gets 404 from that static server.</p><p>What is the cause?</p> {
~The build turned the relative paths into absolute ones; set VITE_API_URL to /api in .env.production#Not quite. Relative paths stay relative in the build. They reach port 4000, which has no API behind it.
~The API now needs a CORS rule for port 4000, because the static server is a different origin from it#Not quite. The requests never reach the API\: the static server answers 404 itself, so CORS is not involved.
~Spring Security rejects requests that come from built files instead of the dev server#Not quite. The 404 comes from the static server; the requests never got to Spring Boot.
=server.proxy exists only in the Vite dev server; production needs its own proxy or one origin for the API#Correct. The built client still requests /api on its own origin, and nothing there forwards it to Spring Boot. In production a reverse proxy or one origin does that job.
}

::html-css-js-final-mcq-30::[html]<p>A Build report button freezes the page for about 3 seconds while a synchronous function runs. <code>button</code> is that button and <code>note</code> is a status paragraph; the developer added a status message.</p><pre><code>button.addEventListener("click", () \=&gt; \{
&nbsp; note.textContent \= "Working...";
&nbsp; buildReport(rows); // synchronous, about 3 seconds
&nbsp; note.textContent \= "Done";
\});</code></pre><p>What does the user see in note after clicking, and why?</p> {
=Only Done, after the freeze\: the page renders only once the click task has finished#Correct. A task runs to completion, then microtasks drain, then the browser may render. By then the text is already Done, so Working... is never painted.
~Working... for about 3 seconds, then Done, because each textContent change is painted at once#Not quite. Changing the DOM does not paint immediately; rendering waits until the running task ends.
~Only Working..., because the browser paints the first change in a task and skips later ones#Not quite. The browser paints the state the DOM is in when the task ends, and by then the last change, Done, has replaced Working....
~Nothing at all, because textContent changes made during a long task are thrown away#Not quite. Both changes are kept; only the last one is visible when the browser finally renders.
}
